  Skills must be exercised to be #enhanceable.  Weapons only count
as exercised if at least 2(?) points of damage are done.  (Some
threshold, anyway.)

  So misses don't count.  And "unskilled" skill level gives a -2
to hit bonus.  OTOH, daggers have a built-in +2 to-hit bonus.
(If you're having trouble improving dagger skill from scratch,
imagine what it's like trying to start improving some other
weapon skill from scratch.)

  Daggers generally only do about two points of damage per hit 
anyway, subject to adjustments for skill, strength, and enchantment.
The adjustments for skill and strength are generally a negative
sum when starting out.

  (The damage done by a thrown dagger may also be decreased after
it's traveled a certain percentage of its total projected flight.)

  Note that means that once you get that first enhancement to dagger
skill, the successful exercises will start snowballing as you've
removed the negative adjustment for hitting/damage for the 
"unskilled" skill level.  (Hitting/damage bonuses will continue
to increase with each #enhancement.)

  The snowballing will continue if you throw daggers to exercise
skill, as the average number of daggers thrown increases with
skill level, up to d3 daggers per throw at Expert level.

  NB: #enhancing to "Expert" skill level from "Skilled"
costs three "skill slots."  You may have better places
to distribute those skill slots early on.  (Start throwing
daggers earlier and from farther away to make up lower
hit/damage bonuses and few daggers thrown.)


(Tip: #adjust your largest dagger stack to 'Q', requivering
is then just 'QQ'.  If you have autoquiver on, adjust any
other stacks to the sequence "RST...", as autoquiver looks
for quiverable items in a-zA-Z order.  #adjust rocks to 't'
so they can be thrown by hitting 'tt')


  To improve the chances of improving skill with daggers:

  - Use plain or elven daggers against small monsters, as there's
    a small damage bonus to those types of daggers against small
    monsters.  (NB: since all daggers do the same damage to large
    monsters, a +1 crude dagger does the same damage to small
    monsters as a +0 elven dagger, but the same +1 crude dagger 
    does 1 more point of damage to a large monster than a +0 elven
    dagger.)

  - Wear Gauntlets of power when using daggers: There's at least
    a +3 to damage done with Str 25.

  - Try to practice with dagger(s) with a positive enchantment.

    - If you have two non-cursed stacks of the same type of dagger,
      wield one of the smaller stack.  (Although I did start one
      game by finding 3 +4 daggers.  That was a nice early game.)

    - Price id your daggers.  Higher prices means more enchantment.
      Non-bones file weapons/armor with a positive enchantment are
      guaranteed non-cursed, btw.

  - Completely unidentified daggers (i.e. completely unknown buc 
    status) can be thrown without risk of getting cursed daggers
    welded to your hand.  Cursed daggers will also occasionally
    slip when thrown.  You'll have to individually #name that
    stack as cursed, though.

  - Throw daggers at lichen, yellow molds, etc. for safe practice.

  - Inside of a fog cloud is the perfect place to practice hitting
    with a wielded dagger:  you can't miss.



  When to enchant a stack of daggers is an interesting question.
You can take advantage of several "multiplier" effects here:

  - Each dagger in the stack gets the same bonus from the scroll
    of enchant weapon, essentially multiplying the enchantment
    from the scroll by the number of daggers.

  - Cancelling all daggers of the same type will give you 
    a larger single stack to enchant.

  - Each uncursed scroll of enchant weapon can have its average
    result doubled by blessing it.

  - A stack of multiple uncursed scrolls of enchant weapon can
    be blessed with a single potion of holy water.

  - Uncursing cursed scrolls of EW can add to your stack of 
    uncursed scrolls to bless.

  - You'll get the enchantment bonus each time you hit a monster
    with one of your enchanted daggers.

  - Elven daggers are better than crude/plain daggers in that
    elven daggers are rust/corrode/fire/whatever proof.  Elven
    daggers are also rarer than crude/plain daggers, so collecting
    a minimal stack will take longer.


  - Silver daggers are more stylish yet, but are incredibly rare.
    Extensive polymorph sessions are generally necessary to get
    a decent stack of silver daggers.


  - Enchanting a stack of daggers larger than you want/need for
    immediate use will give you spares.  (Read Krysia's Krusader
    for the agony of losing some of his "Blessed Deadly Daggers"
    in water.  And the lengths he went to to recover them.)

  - If you don't mean to wield one of your stack of enchanted
    daggers, you'll have to decide whether to enchant your wielded
    weapon before your dagger stack.


  (If you think that total multiplier effect looks impressive:
[!oHW] times [?oEW] times [dagger stack] times [number of hits over
the rest of the game], imagine what the total looks like for
a ranger with a stack of 200 or so arrows fired an average of
2.5 at a time, with poison on them.)



  Fortunately, increasing strength adds an inherent damage bonus
to weapon strikes.  Ditto increasing skill.  Throwing more daggers
per turn also adds damage.  Increasing strength also allows daggers
to be thrown further (i.e. you can start throwing earlier.)  
Increasing player speed also increases the number of daggers thrown
before a monster gets to melee range.


  So even a stack of nine or so "throw-away" (;^) +0 plain daggers
can do a lot of damage if thrown early and often.  I still remember
how surprised I was when a watch-captain fell to my daggers before
he got to melee range.  Cutting a charging mumak down to size is
also nice.  (Hitting a disenchanter at range with +0 daggers
might be rather a pleasant experience.)


  Now you know why some players value a permanent light source over
a wish.  The farther away you see a monster the sooner you can
start attacking it at range and the less likely the monster will
survive to melee range.  (And even most "lit" levels have dark
corridors.)

  For wizards, a stack of highly enchanted daggers is a nice
last resort for tough monsters with magic resistance or reflection.
Your best attack spells (magic missile, finger of death) won't
do any good.  But nothing is immune to physical attack by highly
enchanted weapons.


